\StatementRecord{night:P2.2}{proved}{02RC}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}

On $q=Q^{12}$, actual extending origins have lifts
\begin{align*}
 o&=\binom{-b\mu s/2}{bs^2/24-bCs/12+l_0s/2+v(bs+U)},\\
 \sigma&=-l_0z_\gamma+\binom{6a\mu v}{vU+aCv-6av^2+6av}.
\end{align*}
The section $\sigma$ extends across the cusp for $r\ne0$, and $o$ extends across the parameter central fibre of the partial filling. Their difference is
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01O5}{}\label{eq:P2F}
 F=\sigma-o=a\binom{\mu(s+12v)/2}{C(s+12v)/12-(s+12v)^2/24+6v}.
\end{equation}
On $q=Q^{12m}$, $r=R^n$, with $m,n$ positive integers,
\begin{align}
 \Delta_QF&=am\gamma-6am^2\delta,\hypertarget{tag-01O6}{}\label{eq:P2FQ}\\
 \Delta_RF&=an\gamma-6an(n-1)\delta.\hypertarget{tag-01O7}{}\label{eq:P2FR}
\end{align}
The radial class modulo $\Z\widehat\delta$ is $-pn\widehat\gamma$. The proposed $+pn\widehat\gamma$ has the opposite sign in the fixed conventions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item The lift descent identities are
\begin{align*}
 \Delta_{s,12}o&=-b\gamma+6(b+l_0)\delta,&\Delta_{v,1}o&=H\delta,\\
 \Delta_{s,12}\sigma&=-12l_0\gamma+78l_0\delta,&\Delta_{v,1}\sigma&=H\delta+a\gamma.
\end{align*}
Every additional term is a lattice period. Both lifts therefore define holomorphic sections of the descended torus torsor. In the cusp chart $\sigma+l_0z_\gamma$ is holomorphic and bounded in $q$ and lies in the smooth toric open orbit at $q=0$. It extends there. Subtracting $o$ makes the vector coordinate invariant under the parameter deck. The zero coordinate extends in the rank-one charts of P2.3 below. This constructs the required second origin.

\item Subtracting gives \eqref{eq:P2F}. Integral shifts of $s$ leave $\mu,C,U$ unchanged; expansion of the square gives \eqref{eq:P2FQ}--\eqref{eq:P2FR}. The general-$N$ origin subtraction and both covered deck differences are checked coefficient by coefficient in
\path{code/current_audits/audit_paper_i/code/adjacent_exact.py}, with output \path{adjacent_exact.output.json}. For $(l_0,l_1,l_2)=(0,1,-1)$, $p=-1$, $a=1$, and \eqref{eq:P2F} is the function printed in the prompt. Its radial class is $+n\widehat\gamma$. For every admissible twist $p\ne0$, and the difference between the two proposed classes is $2pn\widehat\gamma\ne0$ in the free quotient lattice.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

\subsection{The partial parameter filling}
\begin{theorem}\hypertarget{tag-02RD}{}\label{night:P2.3}\label{stmt:P2.3}
\StatementRecord{night:P2.3}{proved}{02RD}{the definitions and constructions stated in the proof}

There is a proper holomorphic partial filling over $(\Pp^1\times\Delta_r)\setminus\{(\infty,0)\}$ with smooth total space and reduced normal-crossing parameter central divisor. Over a regular finite base point its central fibre is a cycle of twelve ruled surfaces. Its component rotations at $0,1,\infty$ are $4l_1,3l_2,-b$ modulo $12$, with component cover
\[
 y^{12}=t^{4l_1}(t-1)^{3l_2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item On a simply connected finite-base chart subtract a local origin with $v$-difference $H\delta$, quotient by $\delta$, and put $W=e^{-2\pi i\eta_2}$, $z=\eta_1$. The remaining lattice transformations are
\begin{align*}
 (z,W)&\mapsto(z+1,W),\\
 (z,W)&\mapsto(z+\tau,e^{-2\pi i\mu}W),\\
 (z,W)&\mapsto(z+6\mu,d(t)r^{12}W),
\end{align*}
with $d$ a nonzero holomorphic unit. The first two give the complement of the zero section of a degree-zero line bundle over $\C/(\Z+\tau\Z)$. Use the infinite chain of node charts
\begin{equation}\hypertarget{tag-01O9}{}\label{eq:P2chain}
 X_i=W/r^i,\quad Y_i=r^{i+1}/W,\quad X_iY_i=r\qquad(i\in\Z).
\end{equation}
Adjacent charts are identified on $Y_i\ne0$ by $X_{i+1}=Y_i^{-1}$, $Y_{i+1}=rY_i$. The charts and their inverse overlaps give an infinite projective-line chain at $r=0$. Unit multiplication on $W$ multiplies $X_i,Y_i$ inversely, so all elliptic line-bundle transitions extend. The third period shifts the index by twelve, translates the elliptic coordinate by $6\mu$, and multiplies by $d$. Its quotient has the stated twelve-cycle.

\item The total node chart is smooth by eliminating $r=X_iY_i$, and its central divisor is reduced NC. On a compact smaller finite-base chart the elliptic lattices have uniformly bounded compact fundamental parallelograms. Choose middle annuli and node discs using upper and lower bounds for $d$ and the finitely many transition units. Twelve consecutive indices give a compact fundamental cover over a closed smaller parameter disc. The overlap formulas have closed graphs on that cover, and the positive index translation separates nonadjacent charts. Thus the quotient is Hausdorff and proper. At $r\ne0$ it is exactly the original torus quotient.

\item At root point $j$, addition of $v_j/m_j$ multiplies $W$ by a factor containing $r^{12l_j/m_j}$ and rotates the cycle by $12l_j/m_j$. Every nonidentity power moves the cycle, because $3\nmid l_1$ and $l_2$ is odd. The full finite action is free on the central divisor, including its intersection strata. Its quotient is smooth and preserves the reduced NC parameter divisor. Logarithmic gluing translations differ on overlaps by holomorphic units and integral chain shifts, which extend by the same formulas. The rotations give the cyclic cover; the third is their negative sum and equals $p$ modulo $12$.

\item We glue this finite-base filling to the prescribed cusp filling over $r\ne0$ by the actual translation $\sigma-o$. P2.2 identifies the overlap with the original family. Properness and the Hausdorff property are local on the displayed target, so the glued map has both. The local equations prove flatness over the parameter disc.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

